# The link operator

The link operator is the three-part form

```hql
[[alice]] -> [[bob]] {
    relation: parent
    confidence: 0.9
}
```

`->` constructs an edge, the brackets on either side name its endpoints, and
`{..}` is the data carried on it. A bracket pair on its own is not this operator:
`[[bob]]` is a **doc reference** and asserts nothing.

## `[[..]]` is a doc reference

`[[..]]` written around a name references a document in the current namespace:

```hql
[[alice]]              // a name in this namespace
[[people/alice]]       // a name qualified by module
[[alice#Bio]]          // a heading fragment
[[alice#^claim]]       // an anchored block
```

The referenced document can be in any format the vault holds — Markdown, HMD,
JSON, YAML, HTML. A reference addresses a document, and format is something the
document has, not something the reference selects. Nor does it hand back a
[[card-type|Card]]: card-ness is a knowledge property established by a checked
narrowing, never by the name that points at the target. See [[doc-type]] for what
a document is and [[card-type]] for the vault that resolves the name in it.

Inside the brackets is a name, not an expression: the operator has no variable
form, and addressing a target whose name is computed at runtime needs its own
spelling. Display text is HMD's, not HQL's — `[[alice|Alice B.]]` references the
same document as `[[alice]]`, and whether HQL source accepts the pipe form at all
is open, since `|` is the pipe operator (see [[pipes]]).

## An authored link has an implicit source

In a document body, a bare reference *is* an edge, because the document it sits
in supplies the missing endpoint. Written in `alice`:

```hql
[[bob]] {
    color: red
    relation: parent
}
```

means

```hql
[[alice]] -> [[bob]] {
    color: red
    relation: parent
}
```

`[[this]]` is the source, and `[[this]]` in `alice` is `[[alice]]`. This is what
gives a reference two readings without giving it two meanings. As a value,
`x = [[bob]]` is the reference. As an authored link, `[[bob]]` is that edge, and
`[[bob]]` with no braces is the same edge with empty data. Uplinks and downlinks
are the two directions of traversal over exactly these edges — see [[doc-type]].

## Authoring one adds it to the vault graph

The expansion is not only a reading. An authored link **adds the edge to the
vault's graph**, and the vault is where uplinks, downlinks and `cards` already
come from.

If the edge is already there, the write is an update rather than a second edge:
the data in the braces is merged into the annotation the edge already carries.
So the two links

```hql
[[bob]] { relation: parent }
[[bob]] { confidence: 0.9 }
```

leave one edge from `alice` to `bob` annotated with both keys, not two edges
side by side.

Three things follow, and they are consequences rather than separate decisions:

- **An edge is identified by its endpoints.** One `alice -> bob` edge exists, and
  writing the link again addresses it. A vault that needed two distinct edges
  between the same pair — two different claims, kept apart — could not express
  it this way; it would need an edge identity beyond the pair, which is what a
  `RelationCard` supplies.
- **The annotation accumulates.** Data arrives from every place the link is
  authored, so the annotation on an edge is not necessarily any one author's
  statement.
- **Forward links still work.** The target need not exist, because the edge is
  keyed by the reference, not by a resolved document.

Open: whether the merge is per key at the top level or deep through the tree,
and what happens when two authored links give the same key different values. A
last-write-wins rule would make the annotation depend on vault traversal order,
which is the same objection that makes `cards` ordering a live question — see
[[card-type]]. Retaining both values is the reading consistent with knowledge
keeping conflicting claims, and that argues the conflict should surface rather
than resolve silently.

Also open: whether `[[this]]` is a keyword a program may write, and whether an
edge constructed in an HQL cell asserts into the graph the way an authored one
does. Constructing a value does not persist a document, so a cell that wrote
edges into the vault would be performing an effect, and that has to be explicit.

## `{..}` is ordinary data

The braces are not graph syntax. They are the [[data-type|Data]] attached to the
edge, and the keys in them are the author's:

```hql
Edge {
    source: [[alice]]
    target: [[bob]]
    data: {
        relation: parent
        confidence: 0.9
    }
}
```

`relation`, `color` and `confidence` are entries in a tree, not grammar. Nothing
in the core knows what `relation: parent` means, which is what keeps the edge
form from turning every vocabulary a vault invents into a language keyword — the
same reason the header is `Data` rather than a declared record.

## The graph model behind it

The edge types belong to the graph extension, not to the core:

```hql
type Edge[S, T] {
    source: S
    target: T
    data: Data
}

type Graph[N, E]
```

HMD specializes them for documents, and the knowledge extension specializes them
independently for meaning:

```hql
type Link[S, T]     <: Edge[S, T]
type HmdGraph       <: Graph[Card, Link]

type Relation[S, T] <: Edge[S, T]
type KnowledgeGraph <: {Knowledge, Graph[Concept, Relation]}
```

That separation is the point. `[[alice]] -> [[project-x]]` is an edge in the
document graph; a semantic claim such as `WorksOn(alice, project_x)` is an edge
in a knowledge graph. Both render through the same graph machinery without being
treated as the same object. The layering is core → graph → knowledge, and HMD
sits beside them as one source of documents.

## Data on an edge is not a semantic relation

An edge carries data; that is what `{..}` is for. What it does not do is make the
edge a claim. `relation: parent` is an author's entry in a tree, and reading it
as a typed relationship is an interpretation that has to be performed and
retained — see [[hql/knowledge]].

So a link **can** carry data. What it cannot do is acquire identity or assert
meaning by carrying it. When a relationship needs identity, provenance,
evidence, discussion or incoming links of its own, it should stop being an edge
and become a `RelationCard`: an edge is cheap and anonymous, while a card is a
document other documents can point at — see [[card-type]] for the two kinds of
card and why this one takes no node position.

## Failure

Resolution can fail in HMD's own ways: a target that does not exist, a fragment
missing from a document that does, a name matching several documents, or a
target escaping the namespace root. HMD treats an unresolved link as a *warning*
by design — writing forward to a document that has not been written yet is
permitted, and the diagnostic report is a to-do list. HQL must not turn that into
a hard error by accident.

The edge form makes this concrete rather than settling it: an edge can be
constructed over an endpoint that does not resolve, and the endpoint is the
reference, not the document. Whether a reference used where a document is wanted
yields an `Option`, a value carrying its unresolved state, or a diagnostic is the
largest open question here. Since absence is `Option` and there is no null, the
reading hardest to defend is the one that quietly yields a `Doc`.

Also open: whether `<-` exists as the mirrored form, how `->` binds against `|`,
and whether an endpoint may be an expression rather than a bracketed name.

See [[doc-type]] for uplinks and downlinks, [[card-type]] for the vault,
[[hql/knowledge]] for relation cards, [[data-type]] for the edge's data and
[[hql/design-status]] for what remains undecided.
